    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After the 1920‑27 construction of Lock and Dam No. 4, Lower Burrell became a commercial hub, and the lock is now on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        18,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.experiencepa.com/lower-burrell/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1852 Judge Jeremiah Murry Burrell petitioned to create Lower Burrell Township, carving it out of Allegheny Township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityoflowerburrell.com/211/City-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1879 split formed Lower Burrell and Upper Burrell; later, New Kensington and Arnold were once part of Lower Burrell Township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Burrell%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lower Burrell’s landscape ranges from flat lowlands in Bon Air to steep cliffs near Jacks Island, a 0.6‑mile river island north of Braeburn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Burrell%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The region was originally part of the Iroquois hunting reserves before European settlement began in the 1760s.",
